{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CecwQ9FJkZ58orgf7DSH6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Suite is green: `200 passed, 276 skipped, 166 subtests passed`, no tests removed or skipped.\n\nWhat was actually happening: the failures weren't flaky/infra tests — they exposed a real parsing bug in `pathspec`'s gitignore glob-to-regex translator. POSIX bracket classes like `[[:xdigit:]]` weren't recognized, so the parser mistook the class's internal `:]` for the bracket's closing `]`, truncating the expression and leaving broken, warning-triggering regex (`Possible nested set`) that also silently mismatched other patterns. Deleting/skipping the test would have shipped that bug — anyone using `[[:alpha:]]`-style patterns in a `.gitignore` would have gotten silently wrong matches.\n\nFix, in `pathspec/patterns/gitignore/base.py`:\n- Added a `_POSIX_CLASSES` table mapping class names to git wildmatch's ASCII-only ranges (e.g. `xdigit` → `0-9A-Fa-f`).\n- Made the bracket-expression scanner skip over `[:name:]` sequences instead of stopping at their internal `]`.\n- Added `_expand_posix_classes()` to translate recognized classes and raise `_RangeError` for unknown/malformed ones (`[[:bogus:]]`, `[[:Alpha:]]`, `[[:^alpha:]]`, etc.), which the existing pattern-discard path already handles correctly.\n\nNo test changes were needed or made."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":237,"cache_read_input_tokens":78464,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":237},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54e9d7c1-1345-5e2d-ba67-93201e1134e8","uuid":"89561341-eede-487b-8d5f-396e39adffe3","timestamp":"2026-09-01T15:15:13.196Z","request_id":"req_011CecwQ8PDX1irbWiJkuzAa"}
